\section{Default arguments for matching courses and users}

The first argument we need is to identify a set of courses.
For this we might also have to identify a set of registers.
We'll use the argument and option defined in the [[cli.courses]] module.
We'll default to all courses ([[.*]]) in the register of the [[mine]] command.
<<argument for matching courses>>=
course_regex: Annotated[str, coursescli.course_arg_regex]
<<option for matching registers, default to mine>>=
register: Annotated[str, coursescli.register_opt_regex] = coursescli.MINE
@

Then we can create a list of course--configuration pairs.
We delegate to [[coursescli.get_courses_configs]] to avoid duplicating
the iteration and error handling logic.
Note that this module names the argument [[course_regex]] rather than
[[course]], but the helper accepts either form---it's simply a regex
string.
<<set list [[courses]] to ((course, register), config)-pairs>>=
courses = coursescli.get_courses_configs(course_regex, register)
if not courses:
  sys.exit(1)
@


\section{Looking up TA personal data}

We want to get TA personal data from LADOK.
Sometimes it's sufficient with data from Canvas, but the Canvas data is a
prerequisite for the LADOK data.
We'll do this by using the [[ladok3]] and [[canvaslms]] modules.
Both reuse credentials already configured via their respective CLI tools
([[ladok login]] and [[canvaslms login]]).
<<imports>>=
<<import and set up Canvas>>
<<define [[get_ladok_session]]>>
@

\subsection{Setting up LADOK}

Previously, we had to manually load credentials from a config file and
construct a [[LadokSession]].
Since version 5.12, [[ladok3]] provides a module-level lazy session via
[[ladok3.session]] that handles credential loading and session creation
internally.
That attribute is the LADOK end of the delegation described in the
chapter introduction: behind it, [[ladok3]] consults the environment, its
own configuration file and the system keyring, in an order we neither
know nor are entitled to encode.

The dependency is correspondingly narrow, and narrower than the Canvas
one.
There we import [[canvaslms.cli.login]] explicitly because we call into
that submodule ourselves; here we touch nothing but an attribute of the
top-level package, and [[ladok3]] pulls in its own [[cli]] submodule when
it needs it.
So [[import ladok3]] is the whole of what we depend on, which also means
a broken sub-dependency of [[ladok3]] surfaces as an [[ImportError]]
raised from inside the attribute access --- still \enquote{there is no
LADOK here}, and handled as such below.
<<define [[get_ladok_session]]>>=
def get_ladok_session():
  """Return the LADOK session, or None if unavailable."""
  try:
    import ladok3

    return ladok3.session
  <<handle ladok setup errors>>

  return None
@

Two failures are expected here.
The first is the familiar one: [[ladok3]] can't be imported at all, which
means the LADOK extras aren't installed and there is nothing to log in
to.

The second is the absence of credentials.
[[ladok3]] signals that by raising [[RuntimeError]] from the
module-level [[__getattr__]] that serves [[ladok3.session]], with a
message that already names [[ladok login]].
(The Canvas session below follows the same pattern: there it is
[[canvaslms]]'s own [[CanvasCredentialsError]].)
The rule is the same for both: we answer the library's own
\enquote{nothing here} with a warning and a [[None]] session, and we
answer nothing else.

Catching [[RuntimeError]] is therefore not the old [[except Exception]]
in disguise, even though [[ladok3]] happens, today, to funnel everything
its credential resolution can raise into that one exception.
What we write down is the contract we rely on, not the implementation we
are installed against.
Should a later release let a decryption failure or a network error out
unwrapped, [[except Exception]] would absorb it and go on advising
[[ladok login]] --- the same lie the Canvas code used to tell, and the
same reason its bug survived so long.
The narrow catch lets such an error reach the user instead, which is the
whole point of this section.
<<handle ladok setup errors>>=
except ImportError as err:
  logging.warning(
    f"Can't import ladok3, not using LADOK data: {err}")
except RuntimeError as err:
  logging.warning(
    f"Can't load LADOK credentials, run `ladok login`: {err}")
@

Verifying this needs a LADOK that isn't there, so we stub [[ladok3]] in
[[sys.modules]] the way the Canvas tests stub [[canvaslms]].
The stub reproduces only the interface we rely on: a [[session]]
attribute served by a module-level [[__getattr__]], which either hands
back a session or raises.
Behind it sits a replaceable [[get_session]], and that is where each test
decides what [[ladok3]] found --- so no test can come to depend on how
[[ladok3]] resolves anything, any more than the Canvas tests can.
The tests below read the warnings we log, and [[caplog]] wants a level
constant for that, so the test module brings in [[logging]].
<<test imports>>=
import logging
@

Unlike the Canvas fixture, this one has no cached session to clear.
The cache that matters lives inside [[ladok3]] rather than in this
module, and each test gets a fresh stub --- which doubles as a check that
we have not quietly started caching sessions on our side.
<<test setup>>=
@pytest.fixture
def ladok_stubs(monkeypatch):
  """Replaces ladok3 with a stub whose session the test controls."""
  import sys
  import types

  ladok3 = types.ModuleType("ladok3")

  def get_session():
    raise RuntimeError("No LADOK credentials found. "
                       "Run `ladok login` first.")

  ladok3.get_session = get_session

  def module_getattr(name):
    if name != "session":
      raise AttributeError(name)
    return ladok3.get_session()

  ladok3.__getattr__ = module_getattr

  monkeypatch.setitem(sys.modules, "ladok3", ladok3)

  return types.SimpleNamespace(ladok3=ladok3)
@

With credentials in place we must hand back exactly what [[ladok3]] gave
us, and not a session of our own making:
<<test functions>>=
def test_ladok_session_comes_from_ladok3(ladok_stubs):
  """The session is whatever ladok3 hands back, unmodified."""
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli

  ladok_stubs.ladok3.get_session = lambda: "ladok session for dbosk"

  assert hrcli.get_ladok_session() == "ladok session for dbosk"
@

Without credentials there is no session, and the warning has to name the
command that fixes it.
The stub raises the message [[ladok3]] really raises, and that message
mentions [[ladok login]] too --- so the test asserts our whole phrasing
rather than the two words both share.
Otherwise it would pass just as happily on a version of the code that
merely forwarded [[ladok3]]'s text, which is what the catch-all used to
do:
<<test functions>>=
def test_ladok_session_without_credentials_warns(ladok_stubs, caplog):
  """Absent credentials yield no session, but say what to do."""
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli

  with caplog.at_level(logging.WARNING):
    assert hrcli.get_ladok_session() is None

  assert "Can't load LADOK credentials, run `ladok login`" in caplog.text
@

A missing [[ladok3]] is the other expected failure, and it must still
degrade rather than crash: plenty of nytid runs never touch LADOK.
<<test functions>>=
def test_ladok_session_without_ladok3_warns(monkeypatch, caplog):
  """A missing ladok3 degrades to no session instead of crashing."""
  import sys
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli

  monkeypatch.setitem(sys.modules, "ladok3", None)

  with caplog.at_level(logging.WARNING):
