\subsection{Setting up Canvas}

We also need to query user information from Canvas to find the LADOK ID.
So we should also set up Canvas.
We'll create a function to lazily set up a [[canvas_session]] object.
This way, we only try to load credentials when they're actually needed.

The delegation principle from the chapter introduction becomes concrete
here.
[[canvaslms.get_canvas]] is the library's public entry point for
programs other than its own command line, and it does everything that
lies between \enquote{the user has run [[canvaslms login]]} and \enquote{a
working client}: it resolves a server and a token from whatever sources
[[canvaslms]] currently supports, turns the stored server name into a
URL, installs its retry policy, and restores the encrypted cache that
the [[canvaslms]] command itself keeps.
We deliberately know none of these steps, which are implementation
details of [[canvaslms]] and free to change between releases.
What we get back is a client, and that client is the session.

How much of this we should leave to [[canvaslms]] is a lesson we learnt
twice, and both earlier versions of this function looked almost right.
The first called [[canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials]] to learn
\emph{whether} there were credentials, but then built the session from
the environment variables [[CANVAS_SERVER]] and [[CANVAS_TOKEN]], read
out of [[os.environ]].
That works only when the environment happens to be the source
[[canvaslms]] picked.
With the credentials in the system keyring or in [[canvaslms]]'s own
configuration file, those variables are unset and the lookup raised
[[KeyError]], which a catch-all turned into a [[None]] session and the
callers into advice to run [[canvaslms login]] --- a login that had
already been done.

The second version fixed that by passing the pair returned by
[[load_credentials]] straight to [[canvasapi.Canvas]].
The source was now right, but the pair is the raw material for a
session, not a description of one.
[[canvaslms login]] stores the server the way a person types it,
[[canvas.kth.se]], and [[canvaslms]] prefixes [[https://]] before it ever
reaches [[canvasapi]].
We skipped that step, so every lookup failed with \enquote{Invalid URL
'canvas.kth.se/api/v1/courses': No scheme supplied}, one warning per
user.
Asking for the finished client removes this whole class of mismatch: we
no longer perform any step of the session setup ourselves, so there is
no step left for us to get subtly different from [[canvaslms]].
<<import and set up Canvas>>=
# Global variable to cache the canvas session
_canvas_session = None

def get_canvas_session():
  """Lazily initialize and return the Canvas session."""
  global _canvas_session
  if _canvas_session is not None:
    return _canvas_session

  <<import [[canvaslms]], or return without a session>>

  try:
    _canvas_session = canvaslms.get_canvas()
  <<handle canvas setup errors>>

  return _canvas_session
@

Taking over the cache has consequences beyond speed, and they are what
we want.
The session [[get_canvas]] returns is the same one the [[canvaslms]]
command would use: it is read from the same encrypted file, keyed on the
same server and token, and [[canvaslms]] writes it back when the process
exits.
So an [[nytid hr]] run starts from the courses and users that
[[canvaslms]] has already fetched, and leaves behind what it fetched
itself.
The price is the one [[canvaslms]] already pays: two processes running
at the same time each write back their own copy, and the last one to exit
wins.
That only loses cached data, which is fetched again on the next run.

If [[canvaslms]] isn't installed at all, the Canvas extras are missing
and there is nothing to log in to, so we say so and carry on without a
session.
We import it in a block of its own, separate from the call, because only
a failure to import [[canvaslms]] itself means that.
An [[ImportError]] from inside [[get_canvas]] would mean that
[[canvaslms]] is installed but broken, and that must not be passed off
as a missing installation.
<<import [[canvaslms]], or return without a session>>=
try:
  import canvaslms
except ImportError as err:
  logging.warning(f"Can't import Canvas: {err}")
  return None
@

Two kinds of failure are expected from [[get_canvas]].
The first is that there are no credentials anywhere, which
[[canvaslms]] signals with its own [[CanvasCredentialsError]].
That is not an error, it is the normal state of a fresh installation, so
we log the one action that fixes it and leave the session as [[None]]
for the callers to handle.
The second is that [[canvaslms]]'s configuration file exists but can't
be read, say for lack of permission.
[[canvaslms]] already treats a missing or malformed file as an empty
configuration, so the [[OSError]] is all that is left, and we interpret
it exactly as we interpret a missing login, namely as \enquote{no
credentials}.

Everything else propagates.
An exception from [[get_canvas]] that is neither of these is a genuine
bug or an outage, and turning it into \enquote{run [[canvaslms login]]}
tells the user to fix something that isn't broken.
An early [[except Exception]] here did precisely that, which is how the
environment-variable bug above survived unnoticed for so long.
<<handle canvas setup errors>>=
except canvaslms.CanvasCredentialsError:
  logging.warning("Can't load Canvas credentials, run `canvaslms login`")
  _canvas_session = None
except OSError as err:
  logging.warning(f"Can't load Canvas credentials: {err}")
  _canvas_session = None
@

Verifying all this needs a Canvas that isn't there, so we stub
[[canvaslms]] in [[sys.modules]] before [[get_canvas_session]] imports
it.
A stub, rather than a patch of the real package, keeps the tests honest
about the delegation: the fixture exposes nothing but [[get_canvas]] and
[[CanvasCredentialsError]], so no test can come to depend on how
[[canvaslms]] builds a session.
It also keeps the tests away from the user's real [[canvaslms]] cache,
which the real [[get_canvas]] would read and, at exit, overwrite.
The fixture also has to keep one test's Canvas out of the next, and three
things would otherwise carry it across: the cached [[_canvas_session]]
and the [[cachetools]] caches behind the two lookups below.
[[monkeypatch]] handles the first on its own, since it restores what it
patched once the test ends.
The lookup caches have no such owner --- they are module-level
dictionaries keyed on the regexes alone, so an entry computed against one
stub answers just as readily for whatever comes next.

That is why the fixture [[yield]]s rather than returns: it clears them on
the way in \emph{and} on the way out.
Clearing only on the way in would protect every test that uses the
fixture and none that follows it, leaving the last one's stub-derived
results in a cache the real lookups share for the rest of the session.
<<test setup>>=
@pytest.fixture
def canvas_stubs(monkeypatch):
  """Replaces canvaslms with a recording stub."""
  import sys
  import types
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli

  canvaslms = types.ModuleType("canvaslms")

  class CanvasCredentialsError(RuntimeError):
    pass

  def get_canvas():
    raise CanvasCredentialsError("No hostname or token")

  canvaslms.CanvasCredentialsError = CanvasCredentialsError
  canvaslms.get_canvas = get_canvas
  canvaslms.cli = types.ModuleType("canvaslms.cli")

  for name, module in [("canvaslms", canvaslms),
                       ("canvaslms.cli", canvaslms.cli)]:
    monkeypatch.setitem(sys.modules, name, module)

  def clear_lookup_caches():
    hrcli.get_canvas_courses.cache_clear()
    hrcli.get_canvas_users.cache_clear()

  monkeypatch.setattr(hrcli, "_canvas_session", None)
  clear_lookup_caches()

  yield types.SimpleNamespace(canvaslms=canvaslms)

  clear_lookup_caches()
@

