\subsection{Looking up usernames in Canvas and LADOK}

Now that we have a (hopefully) working [[canvas_session]] and
[[ladok_session]], we can look up a username in Canvas and then from that user
object we can look up the LADOK data.
Both getters return [[None]] when there is nothing to authenticate with,
and the consumers below turn that into a [[RuntimeError]] telling the
user to run [[canvaslms login]].
That is honest advice now only because the session is built from the
credentials [[canvaslms]] actually resolved; a session we drop on the
floor while valid credentials exist makes the same message a lie.
To do this, we'll introduce two helper functions:
One to look up the username in Canvas, to get a Canvas user object.
Another to look up the LADOK ID from the Canvas user object.

Canvas is super slow, so we'll cache the results.
Then we can cache big searches.
We'll create several helper functions that we cache.
We'll start with listing courses.
<<helper functions>>=
@cachetools.cached(cache={})
def get_canvas_courses(course_regex):
  """
  Returns a list of Canvas course objects matching the given course_regex.
  """
  <<filter out [[courses]] in Canvas from [[course_regex]]>>
  return courses
<<imports>>=
import cachetools
@

Now we introduce the helper function to look up a usernames in Canvas.
We'll cache this one as well.
Then we can add a function to search for specific users that can get all
users using this function and cache the result.
<<helper functions>>=
@cachetools.cached(cache={})
def get_canvas_users(username_regex, course_regex):
  """
  Returns a list of Canvas user objects matching the given username_regex.
  Searches for username_regex in the courses matching course_regex.
  """
  courses = get_canvas_courses(course_regex)
  <<filter [[users]] by looking for [[username_regex]] in [[courses]]>>
  return users
@

All the filtering we want to do has already been implemented in [[canvaslms]].
So we can simply call the right library functions.
<<filter out [[courses]] in Canvas from [[course_regex]]>>=
canvas_session = get_canvas_session()
if canvas_session is None:
  raise RuntimeError("Canvas session not available, run `canvaslms login`")
import canvaslms.cli.courses
courses = list(canvaslms.cli.courses.filter_courses(canvas_session,
                                                    course_regex))
<<filter [[users]] by looking for [[username_regex]] in [[courses]]>>=
import canvaslms.cli.users
users = list(canvaslms.cli.users.filter_users(courses,
                                              username_regex))
@

Neither import is guarded, and that is the second half of the silent
failures this section is about.
Both used to catch [[ImportError]] and put an empty list in place of the
result, which made a broken Canvas installation indistinguishable from a
working one that matched nothing.
An empty list is not a failure the callers can notice, it is an answer
they act on: [[get_canvas_user]] turns it into \enquote{can't find dbosk
in Canvas}, which reads as a TA who never joined the course, and the run
continues quietly to the next one.

Two things make the empty list wrong here rather than merely unhelpful.
The first is that the line just above already treats the absence of
Canvas as fatal --- a [[None]] session becomes a [[RuntimeError]] --- so
one function gave the same condition two opposite outcomes.
The second is that the [[None]]-session branch has already run by the
time we reach the import.
[[get_canvas_session]] imports [[canvaslms]] itself and returns [[None]]
if it is missing, so an absent Canvas installation never gets this far.
What is left for [[ImportError]] to mean here is much narrower and much
stranger: [[canvaslms]] is installed, it has handed us a session, and one
of its submodules will not load.
That is a broken installation, exactly the \enquote{unexpected} of the
chapter introduction, and so it propagates.

We let [[ImportError]] out unchanged rather than translating it into a
[[RuntimeError]] the way the session check does, because that check has
advice to add ([[canvaslms login]]) and here we have none to give.
[[ImportError]] already names the submodule that would not load, which is
the one thing anyone debugging this needs, and wrapping it would trade a
precise message for a vaguer one.
The callers need no change either way: each one wraps these lookups in a
warning naming the user it was looking up, so the failure becomes visible
without aborting a run over a whole course's TAs.

The Canvas stubs from the session tests are enough to check this.
We register the submodule as [[None]] in [[sys.modules]], which is how
imports are spelled out of existence there; letting the import fail
