Once we have the Canvas user object, the LADOK student is one call away:
Canvas stores the student's LADOK UID as the user's [[integration_id]],
and [[get_student]] accepts exactly that.
We don't need to cache this function since LADOK already caches the
results and is very fast.

What [[get_student]] returns, though, is a promise rather than a record.
[[ladok3]]'s [[Student]] is lazy: constructing it sends nothing to LADOK,
and the first read of an attribute such as [[personnummer]] is what
fetches the data.
So a student LADOK doesn't know does not fail here, it fails wherever the
object is first printed.
In [[nytid hr users --detailed]] that is the [[str(user_obj)]] that
renders the row, outside the [[try]] that turns a failed lookup into a
warning, and so one unknown TA used to abort the listing of everyone.
An [[except KeyError]] here once looked like it handled lookup failures,
but nothing in the constructor can raise one, and LADOK reports an
unknown student with its own [[LadokError]]s rather than a
[[KeyError]].

We therefore make the lookup happen here, inside [[get_ladok_user]],
where the callers already catch failures.
<<helper functions>>=
def get_ladok_user(canvas_user):
  """
  Takes a Canvas user object and returns a LADOK student object.

  The student's personal data is fetched before returning, so a student
  LADOK doesn't know raises here rather than when the object is printed.
  """
  ladok_session = get_ladok_session()
  if ladok_session is None:
    raise RuntimeError("LADOK session not available, run `ladok login`")

  <<let [[ladok_id]] be [[canvas_user]]'s LADOK ID>>
  student = ladok_session.get_student(ladok_id)
  <<fetch [[student]]'s personal data now>>
  return student
@

Not every Canvas user has an [[integration_id]].
A teacher who has never studied at the university, for instance, has no
LADOK record for Canvas to point to.
Passed on as [[None]], it made [[ladok3]] fail deep in its personnummer
parsing with \enquote{expected string or bytes-like object, got
'NoneType'}, which says nothing about the actual problem.
We name the actual problem instead.
<<let [[ladok_id]] be [[canvas_user]]'s LADOK ID>>=
ladok_id = getattr(canvas_user, "integration_id", None)
if not ladok_id:
  raise ValueError(f"{canvas_user} has no LADOK ID "
                   "(integration_id) in Canvas")
@

Any attribute of the personal record would force the fetch, and we read
[[personnummer]] because the callers need it anyway: it is the first
thing [[str]] prints and what the time sheets are made out to.
Since [[student]] was created from a LADOK UID, it doesn't know its
personnummer yet, so reading it is guaranteed to go to LADOK.
The same request brings the first and last names, so nothing the callers
read later goes to LADOK again.
[[Student.pull]] would also force the fetch, but it additionally fetches
every course registration of the student, which we never use.
<<fetch [[student]]'s personal data now>>=
student.personnummer
@

Both failures are checked with a stand-in for [[ladok3]]'s [[Student]]
that is lazy in the same way: it records whether anything has been
fetched and fails only on the first attribute read.
A stub session that raises from [[get_student]] would not do, since it
would pass with or without the fix:
<<test setup>>=
class LazyStudent:
  """Mimics ladok3.Student: the record is only fetched on attribute access."""
  def __init__(self, ladok_id, error=None):
    self.ladok_id = ladok_id
    self.error = error
    self.fetched = False

  @property
  def personnummer(self):
    self.fetched = True
    if self.error:
      raise self.error
    return "190001019999"


class LazyLadokSession:
  """A LADOK session handing out LazyStudent objects."""
  def __init__(self, error=None):
    self.error = error
    self.requested = []

  def get_student(self, ladok_id):
    self.requested.append(ladok_id)
    return LazyStudent(ladok_id, self.error)
@

A successful lookup returns a student whose record has already been
fetched:
<<test functions>>=
def test_ladok_user_is_fetched_before_returning(monkeypatch):
  """The LADOK record is loaded inside get_ladok_user."""
  import types
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli

  session = LazyLadokSession()
  monkeypatch.setattr(hrcli, "get_ladok_session", lambda: session)

  student = hrcli.get_ladok_user(
    types.SimpleNamespace(integration_id="uid-1"))

  assert session.requested == ["uid-1"]
  assert student.fetched
@

An unknown student must fail inside [[get_ladok_user]], where the
callers catch it, and not later when the row is printed:
