\subsection{Listing the periods}

Listing the periods is straightforward: we read the config and print
one row per period.

This is the one command in this chapter whose \emph{piped} bytes
change.
It used to write its three columns with [[csv.writer]] and, unlike
every other listing in nytid, it emitted the header row
unconditionally --- so a pipeline had to know to throw the first line
away.
Routing it through [[print_rows]] (\cref{cli.utils.output}) brings it
in line with the rest: the header appears on a terminal, where it
tells the reader what the three columns are, and disappears in a pipe,
where it was only ever an obstacle.

Dropping [[csv.writer]] changes a second, less visible byte.
Its default line terminator is CRLF, so the piped output carried a
stray carriage return at the end of every line; [[print_rows]] ends
lines with a plain newline.
Neither change breaks anything we ship: nothing in the test suite or
in the cron wrappers reads this command's output, which is why it was
safe to fix here and not elsewhere.

<<subcommands>>=
@periods.command(name="list")
def cli_periods_list():
  """
  Lists the periods and their start and end dates.

  Piped, this is one `period<tab>start<tab>end` line per period with
  no header; on a terminal the columns are labelled and aligned.
  """
  print_rows(["Period", "Start", "End"],
             [(period, str(dates["start"]), str(dates["end"]))
              for period, dates in get_all_periods().items()])
@

Let's confirm both halves of that claim --- three tab-separated
fields, and no header line:
