When the [[--xlsx]] option is set, we also emit a planning spreadsheet for the
user.  The sheet only needs a human-readable name and email, both of which
Canvas supplies, so we resolve the user against \emph{Canvas alone} --- we
deliberately do not consult LADOK here.  This is the key difference from the
printed summary: the summary's detailed mode looks the user up in LADOK to
obtain the personnummer, but the planning sheet never shows a personnummer, so
that lookup would be wasted work (and would make the spreadsheet depend on a
[[ladok login]] it does not need).  For the same reason the sheet ignores
[[--no-detailed]]: that flag exists to skip the expensive LADOK lookup, and this
path has none to skip.  We build the per-course rows and write the file under
the name the chosen [[--xlsx-format]] dictates (\cref{sec:xlsx-format}); the
naming itself lives in [[amanuensis_xlsx_filename]], described after the
Canvas lookup below.  The chunk body sits at the loop-body level so
[[notangle]] places it as a sibling of the summary print, not nested inside it.

We write the resolved [[start]] and [[end]], not [[data[0]]] and [[data[1]]].
The two differ under [[--push-start]]: pushing keeps the percentage, so it
shifts [[start]] and [[end]] but deliberately leaves [[data]] untouched (see
\cref{sec:modify-contract-dates}).  The planning sheet must show the contract's
\emph{actual} employment period --- the same dates we just printed --- so it
reads the resolved variables.

We pass [[start.date()]] and [[end.date()]] rather than the dates themselves.
These dates are timezone-aware [[datetime.datetime]] objects, and [[openpyxl]]
rejects a timezone-aware datetime with \enquote{Cannot convert to Excel}.
[[.date()]] yields a plain [[datetime.date]] the cell accepts --- which also
matches what the cell means: an employment period has day resolution, so a
(midnight) time component would be noise.
<<write [[user]]'s planning spreadsheet>>=
if xlsx:
  <<resolve [[user]] to a Canvas display name>>
  <<let [[user_booked_by_course]] be [[booked_by_course]] for [[user]]>>
  course_rows = amanuensis_course_rows(user, user_booked_by_course, courses,
                                       default_course_responsible)
  output = amanuensis_xlsx_filename(xlsx_format, user, sheet_user,
                                    courses, period, start)
  timesheet.make_amanuensis_xlsx(
      name=str(sheet_user),
      email=getattr(sheet_user, "email", "") or "",
      start=start.date(),
      end=end.date(),
      course_rows=course_rows,
      responsible=(default_course_responsible, default_email),
      output=output)
@

The Canvas lookup goes through [[get_canvas_user]], which is backed by the
cached [[get_canvas_users]]; so even when the detailed summary already resolved
this same user earlier in the loop, this second call just re-filters the cache
rather than hitting the network.  If Canvas is unavailable we fall back to the
bare username, mirroring the summary's own fallback --- a sheet headed by the
login is still useful, and we would rather emit it than abort.  We keep this in
its own variable [[sheet_user]] so it does not clobber the [[user_obj]] the
summary path resolved (which, under [[--detailed]], holds the LADOK student).
<<resolve [[user]] to a Canvas display name>>=
try:
  sheet_user = get_canvas_user(user, course_regex)
except Exception as err:
  logging.warning(f"Can't look up {user} in Canvas: {err}")
  sheet_user = user
