\subsection{Carrying the difference into the next contract}%
\label{sec:carry-difference}

A TA rarely works exactly the sessions of their contract.
They take extra sessions, or sessions they were contracted for are
cancelled or taken by someone else.
The time sheets of \cref{WorkingWithTimesheets} report that difference
as \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{mertid}}, positive or negative.
Often it's simpler to fold the difference into the TA's next contract
instead, raising or lowering its percentage, and skip the time sheet.
We do that by letting the bookings reach back into the previous period
while the contract itself covers only the next one:
\begin{minted}{bash}
nytid hr amanuensis create "(datintro|prg[im])25" \
  --period 25/26-2 --start 2025-08-25
\end{minted}
The period sets the contract dates, [[--set-start]] and [[--set-end]],
to period~2; the explicit [[--start]] overrides only the start of the
booking window.
The sessions between the start of the booking window and the start of
the contract are the ones we carry.
(The same goes for an [[--end]] after [[--set-end]], although carrying
from a later period is rarely what we want.)

What we carry must be what the next time sheet would report for those
dates: never more, and less only where a contract can't hold it (see
below).
The contract for period~1 already pays for most of its sessions, so we
mustn't count them again; and a session the TA was paid for on a time
sheet mustn't be paid again either.
So we compute the difference the way [[timesheets generate]] does: the
sessions the TA is booked on now, compared with the events already
accounted for by contracts and reported time sheets
([[accounted_events]], \cref{sec:accounted-events}).
\begin{description}
\item[Added sessions] are booked now but not accounted for.
  They become ordinary events of the new contract.
\item[Removed sessions] are accounted for but no longer booked.
  We store them in the contract's [[removed_events]], and their hours are
  deducted from the contract's hours.
\end{description}
Once stored, the new contract accounts for the difference: its events
cover the added sessions and its [[removed_events]] cancel the removed
ones (\cref{sec:accounted-events}), so the next time sheet for
period~1 reports nothing that a contract can hold.
One kind of session it still reports: a session paid on an earlier time
sheet and since taken off the TA.
A time sheet records only a summary of the session, not the sign-up
sheet row that a contract stores, so we leave it to the next time sheet,
which deducts it as before.
Nothing is paid twice either way.

The contracts that account for events are those the new contract will
\emph{not} replace, that is, those whose periods don't overlap its own
(\cref{contract-filtering}).
This is what makes re-creating a contract work.
Contrast three cases.
\begin{description}
\item[Carrying from period~1 into period~2.]
  The period~1 contract doesn't overlap period~2, so it's kept, and its
  sessions are accounted for: only the difference is carried.
\item[Re-creating the contract for period~2.]
  The old version overlaps the new, so it will be replaced, and what it
  carried isn't accounted for.
  The difference is computed afresh, as if the old version had never
  existed.
\item[Re-creating the contract for period~1 afterwards.]
  The period~2 contract is kept, and it holds sessions from period~1.
  If the new period~1 contract now includes those sessions, they are
  paid twice---or, for removed sessions, deducted twice.
  We can't fix this from the period~1 side without second-guessing the
  period~2 contract, so we warn and ask for the period~2 contract to be
  re-created afterwards; that computes its difference against the new
  period~1 contract.
\end{description}

\subsubsection{The windows}

Without carrying, [[create]] computes all TAs' data in one go, over the
booking window, and drops TAs below the minimum percentage or number of
days.
With carrying, that would judge the minimum over the wrong period.
A TA who works only in period~2 has too few hours for the long window
that reaches back into period~1, and would silently get no contract,
although they have enough for period~2 alone.
So when there is something to carry we compute everyone's data without
thresholds here and leave the judgement to each TA's recomputation over
the contract's own period (\cref{sec:carry-for-each-ta}).
<<let [[amanuensis]] be the TAs' data from [[booked]]>>=
if has_carry_window(start, end, set_start, set_end):
  amanuensis = hr.compute_amanuensis_data(booked,
                                          begin_date=start, end_date=end,
                                          low_percentage=0, min_days=0)
else:
  amanuensis = hr.compute_amanuensis_data(booked,
                                          begin_date=start,
                                          end_date=end)
@

We remember the booking window before the loop over the TAs, because
the loop reuses [[start]] and [[end]] for each TA's contract dates.
<<let [[bookings_start]] and [[bookings_end]] be the booking window>>=
bookings_start, bookings_end = start, end
@

The contract's own period is [[--set-start]] and [[--set-end]] when
given, otherwise the booking window.
There is something to carry only if the booking window extends beyond
that period, and only on a side where the booking window has a bound,
whether from [[--start]] and [[--end]] or from [[--period]].
Without one, the booking window is the whole sign-up sheet; we'd have no
reasonable bound for the contracts' events to compare with, so we keep
the old behaviour of simply counting the bookings.
Note that this includes a [[--set-start]] a few days after the period's
start, as in \cref{sec:periods}: the bookings of those days are now
compared with what is accounted for, rather than counted regardless.
<<helper functions>>=
def has_carry_window(bookings_start, bookings_end, own_start, own_end):
  """
  Returns True if the booking window extends beyond the contract's own
  period on a side where both are given.
  """
  return bool(bookings_start and own_start
              and bookings_start.date() < own_start.date()
              or bookings_end and own_end
              and bookings_end.date() > own_end.date())
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_has_carry_window():
  sep1 = datetime.datetime(2025, 8, 25).astimezone()
  oct27 = datetime.datetime(2025, 10, 27).astimezone()
  jan18 = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 18).astimezone()
  assert has_carry_window(sep1, jan18, oct27, jan18)
  assert not has_carry_window(oct27, jan18, oct27, jan18)
  assert not has_carry_window(None, jan18, oct27, jan18)
  assert has_carry_window(oct27, jan18 + datetime.timedelta(days=1),
                          oct27, jan18)
@

\subsubsection{Computing the difference}

The difference is computed by [[carry_diff]], which returns the rows to
add and the rows to remove.
We compare events in the format of [[summarize_user]], with the same
equality as the time sheets ([[remove_events]]), so that a session counts
as the same session here exactly when it would on a time sheet.
But we return \emph{rows}, not summaries: the contract stores rows, and
its hours are computed from rows with the amanuensis preparation time
(\cref{sec:contract-data}), which differs from the hourly one that
[[summarize_user]] applies.
<<helper functions>>=
def carry_diff(user, booked, bookings_window, own_window,
               contracts, timesheets_dir):
  """
  Returns (added, removed): the rows of sessions outside the contract's
  own period that `user` worked beyond, respectively short of, what
  `contracts` and the time sheets in `timesheets_dir` account for.

  `booked` are the sign-up sheet rows in the booking window; both
  windows are (start, end) pairs of datetimes.
  """
  <<signup machinery imports>>
  bookings_start, bookings_end = bookings_window
  own_start, own_end = own_window

  accounted = accounted_events(user, contracts, timesheets_dir,
                               bookings_start, bookings_end)
  booked_now = summarize_user(user, booked)

  <<let [[added]] be the carried rows not accounted for>>
  <<let [[removed]] be the accounted rows no longer booked>>

  return added, removed
@

The added rows are the bookings outside the contract's period whose
session isn't accounted for.
A row where the TA is only a reserve summarises to nothing and is
skipped.
<<let [[added]] be the carried rows not accounted for>>=
added = []
for row in outside_window(booked, own_start, own_end):
  summary = summarize_user(user, [row])
  if summary and summary[0] not in accounted:
    added.append(row)
@

The removed rows come from the contracts, since a session the TA no
longer is booked on is no longer in [[booked]].
We take the contracts' events in the booking window but outside the
contract's period that are still accounted for and no longer booked.
A session may be in several contracts (an earlier carry), so we keep
each session once.
This relies on [[booked]] covering every course of those contracts: a
session of a course whose sheet we didn't read would look removed.
Ensuring that is the caller's job (\cref{sec:carry-courses}).
<<let [[removed]] be the accounted rows no longer booked>>=
removed = []
removed_summaries = []
contract_rows = [row for contract in contracts
                     for row in contract["events"]]
for row in outside_window(
    sheets.filter_events_by_date(contract_rows,
                                 bookings_start, bookings_end),
    own_start, own_end):
  summary = summarize_user(user, [row])
  if not summary:
    continue
  summary = summary[0]
  if summary in accounted and summary not in booked_now \
      and summary not in removed_summaries:
    removed.append(row)
    removed_summaries.append(summary)
@

The warning above checks rows against a window of dates, which
[[filter_events_by_date]] can't take (it wants datetimes), so we spell the
same inclusive, day-resolution test out.
<<helper functions>>=
def rows_in_window(rows, window):
  """
  Returns the rows whose event lies within `window`, a pair of
  `datetime.date` (inclusive; None means open).
  """
  start, end = window
  return [row for row in rows
          if (start is None
              or start <= datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(row[1]).date())
          and (end is None
               or datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(row[2]).date() <= end)]
@

Finally, the rows outside a window are those that
[[filter_events_by_date]] doesn't keep; reusing it keeps the day
resolution and inclusive bounds identical to the rest of the command.
<<helper functions>>=
def outside_window(rows, start, end):
  """
  Returns the rows in `rows` that are not between `start` and `end`
  (inclusive, day resolution).
  """
  <<signup machinery imports>>
  inside = sheets.filter_events_by_date(rows, start, end)
  return [row for row in rows if row not in inside]
@

The tests of carrying need contracts and sign-up sheet rows.
Throughout, the TA is [[erin]], period~1 is autumn 2025 up to 26
October and period~2 the rest of the autumn.
A contract row is a sign-up sheet row cut to the TA it was created for,
as [[<<filter out [[user_events]] for [[user]]>>]] stores it; a contract
is written to a temporary contract directory the way [[create --period]]
writes it, and [[_contract_dirs]] points the configuration there, as the
[[temp_contracts_dir]] fixture does.
<<test functions>>=
def _write_contract(path, user, created, start, end, events=None,
                    **fields):
  """Writes a contract as `create --period` would, with set dates."""
  contract = {"user": user, "start": start, "end": end,
              "set_start": start, "set_end": end,
              "events": events or []}
  contract.update(fields)
  with open(path / f"{user}.{created}.json", "w") as outfile:
    json.dump(contract, outfile)

