\subsubsection{The contract's hours}\label{sec:contract-data}

With removed sessions, a contract's hours are no longer just the hours
of its events.
[[contract_data]] computes them as the hours of the events minus the
hours of the removed sessions, both with [[compute_amanuensis_data]] so
that the amanuensis preparation time applies to both.
For the removed sessions we turn off the minimum percentage and number
of days: those thresholds judge whether a contract is worth having, not
how many hours a set of sessions amounts to.
The minimum percentage must then be judged again on what remains, since
[[compute_amanuensis_data]] judged it before the deduction.
Both [[create]] and [[show]] use it, so a stored contract shows the same
percentage it was created with.
<<helper functions>>=
def contract_data(user, events, removed_events, start, end, **kwargs):
  """
  Returns `user`'s (start, end, hours) for a contract with `events`,
  deducting the hours of `removed_events`, or None if the contract
  falls below the thresholds of `compute_amanuensis_data`, the minimum
  percentage judged after the deduction.
  `start`, `end` and `kwargs` are passed to `compute_amanuensis_data`.
  """
  <<signup machinery imports>>
  data = hr.compute_amanuensis_data(events, begin_date=start,
                                    end_date=end, **kwargs)
  if user not in data:
    return None
  start, end, hours = data[user]
  if removed_events:
    removed = hr.compute_amanuensis_data(removed_events,
                                         low_percentage=0, min_days=0)
    if user in removed:
      hours -= removed[user][2]
    low_percentage = kwargs.get("low_percentage",
                                hr.AMANUENSIS_MIN_PERCENTAGE)
    if hr.compute_percentage(start, end, hours) < low_percentage:
      return None
  return start, end, hours
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_contract_data_deducts_removed_hours():
  start = datetime.datetime(2025, 10, 27).astimezone()
  end = datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 18).astimezone()
  events = [_row("2025-11-03 10:00", "2025-11-03 12:00"),
            _row("2025-11-10 10:00", "2025-11-10 12:00")]
  removed = [_row("2025-09-08 10:00", "2025-09-08 12:00")]
  full = contract_data("erin", events, [], start, end,
                       low_percentage=0, min_days=0)
  less = contract_data("erin", events, removed, start, end,
                       low_percentage=0, min_days=0)
  one = contract_data("erin", events[:1], [], start, end,
                      low_percentage=0, min_days=0)
  assert full[2] - less[2] == pytest.approx(one[2])
  assert less[:2] == full[:2]
@

\subsubsection{Carrying for each TA}\label{sec:carry-for-each-ta}

Now we can put the parts together in [[create]].
For each TA we determine the contract's own period and which of the TA's
contracts it will not replace.
If there is something to carry, we compute the difference, replace the
TA's bookings by the bookings in the period plus the added rows, and
recompute the TA's data from them.
We recompute with the contract's own period as the forced dates, so that
the minimum percentage is judged over the period the contract actually
covers, not over the longer booking window.
[[user_booked]] then takes the place of [[booked]] for this TA when we
record the contract's events.
<<carry the difference from outside the contract period for [[user]]>>=
user_booked = booked
removed_rows = []
own_start = set_start or bookings_start
own_end = set_end or bookings_end
<<let [[kept_contracts]] be [[user]]'s contracts this one won't replace>>
<<warn about replaced contracts for courses we don't read>>
<<warn about replaced contracts for sessions outside this period>>
<<warn if [[kept_contracts]] carried sessions from this period>>
if has_carry_window(bookings_start, bookings_end, own_start, own_end):
  <<let [[uncovered]] be the kept contracts with courses we didn't read>>
  if uncovered:
    <<warn that we don't carry because of [[uncovered]]>>
    added_rows = removed_rows = []
  else:
    added_rows, removed_rows = carry_diff(user, booked,
                                          (bookings_start, bookings_end),
                                          (own_start, own_end),
                                          kept_contracts,
                                          timesheets_directory())
  <<let [[data]] be [[user]]'s contract data with the carried difference>>
  if not data:
    continue
  amanuensis[user] = data
  <<report the carried difference for [[user]]>>
@

The deduction can leave a contract below the minimum percentage, or
even negative, if the TA dropped more sessions in period~1 than they
take in period~2.
That contract can't be signed, so instead we carry nothing and leave the
difference for the time sheet, as we do when we can't read every course;
carrying only the additions would be wrong for the same reason as there
(\cref{sec:carry-courses}).
If the contract is below the minimum even without the difference, the
TA gets no contract, as without carrying.
<<let [[data]] be [[user]]'s contract data with the carried difference>>=
in_period = sheets.filter_events_by_date(booked, own_start, own_end)
user_booked = in_period + added_rows
data = contract_data(user, user_booked, removed_rows, own_start, own_end)
if not data and (added_rows or removed_rows):
  logging.warning(f"{user}: carrying the difference would leave the "
                  "contract below the minimum percentage or days; "
                  "leaving the difference for the time sheet.")
  user_booked, added_rows, removed_rows = in_period, [], []
  data = contract_data(user, user_booked, [], own_start, own_end)
@

\paragraph{Reading every course of the earlier contracts}%
\label{sec:carry-courses}

The difference is only right if we read the sign-up sheets of every
course the earlier contracts cover.
Say the period~1 contract was created for [[(datintro|prgi)26]] and we
create the period~2 contract for [[prgi26]] alone.
The datintro sessions of the period~1 contract aren't in the sheets we
read, so they would look as if the TA had dropped them all, and we would
deduct them.
Recognising a session's course from its event name doesn't help: the
names rarely carry a course code.
Instead we check each kept contract that has events in the carry window:
its stored [[course_regex]] must match only courses that we read now.
If not, we carry nothing at all for this TA rather than only the added
sessions.
Carrying only the additions isn't safe either: a renamed session shows
up as one added and one removed, and keeping only the added half would
pay for it twice.
A contract that doesn't record its courses can't be checked, so it also
blocks the carry.
<<let [[uncovered]] be the kept contracts with courses we didn't read>>=
uncovered = {}
for contract in kept_contracts:
  in_carry_window = outside_window(
    sheets.filter_events_by_date(
      contract["events"] + contract.get("removed_events", []),
      bookings_start, bookings_end),
    own_start, own_end)
  if in_carry_window:
    missing = uncovered_courses(contract, courses, register)
    if missing is None or missing:
      uncovered[contract["filename"]] = missing
<<warn that we don't carry because of [[uncovered]]>>=
for filename, missing in uncovered.items():
  if missing is None:
    reason = "doesn't record which courses it covers"
  else:
    reason = "also covers " + ", ".join(sorted({course
                                               for course, _ in missing}))
  logging.warning(f"{user}: not carrying the difference: {filename} "
                  f"{reason}. Use a course regex covering all its "
                  "courses.")
@

Contracts store the courses they were created from, as
[[(course, register)]] pairs, and we compare those.
Re-resolving the stored regex instead would depend on the registers we
search now: a contract made from a course in another register would
seem to cover only the courses in ours, and the other course's sessions
would be deducted.
Older contracts have only the regex, and for those re-resolving it in
the same registers as the command's own course argument is the best we
can do.
<<helper functions>>=
def uncovered_courses(contract, courses, register):
  """
  Returns the set of (course, register) pairs that `contract` covers but
  that aren't among `courses`, or None if the contract doesn't record
  its courses.
  """
  if contract.get("courses"):
    covered = {tuple(key) for key in contract["courses"]}
  elif contract.get("course_regex"):
    covered = set(coursescli.get_courses_configs(contract["course_regex"],
                                                 register))
  else:
    return None
  return covered - set(courses)
@

Let's verify both outcomes, stubbing only the course lookup, which reads
the course registers on disk.
<<test functions>>=
def test_uncovered_courses(monkeypatch):
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli
  known = {"(datintro|prgi)26": {("datintro26", "r"): None,
                                 ("prgi26", "r"): None},
           "prgi26": {("prgi26", "r"): None}}
  monkeypatch.setattr(hrcli.coursescli, "get_courses_configs",
                      lambda regex, register: known[regex])
  p1 = {"course_regex": "(datintro|prgi)26"}
  assert uncovered_courses(p1, known["prgi26"], "mine") == \
    {("datintro26", "r")}
  assert uncovered_courses(p1, known["(datintro|prgi)26"], "mine") == set()
  assert uncovered_courses({"course_regex": None}, {}, "mine") is None
  other_register = {"course_regex": "prgi26",
                    "courses": [["prgi26", "r"], ["datintro26", "other"]]}
  assert uncovered_courses(other_register, known["prgi26"], "mine") == \
    {("datintro26", "other")}
@

The contracts this one won't replace are those whose periods don't
overlap its own, the same rule [[get_valid_contracts]] will apply once
the new contract is stored.
We compute the new contract's window with the same rule as for stored
contracts ([[specified_window]], \cref{sec:contract-dates}).
<<let [[kept_contracts]] be [[user]]'s contracts this one won't replace>>=
own_window = specified_window(own_start, own_end,
                              sheets.filter_events_by_TA(user, booked))
user_contracts = get_user_contracts(user)
kept_contracts = [contract for contract in user_contracts
                  if not windows_overlap(contract_window(contract),
                                         own_window)]
@

A TA has one valid contract per period, so the new contract replaces an
earlier one for the same period even if that one was made for other
courses---by us or, since the contract directory may be shared, by a
colleague.
The sessions of those other courses are then no longer accounted for,
and would show up on the next time sheet.
That may be intended (the new contract should then include those
courses), but it should never happen silently.
<<warn about replaced contracts for courses we don't read>>=
for contract in user_contracts:
  if contract in kept_contracts:
    continue
  missing = uncovered_courses(contract, courses, register)
  if missing:
    logging.warning(
      f"{user}: this contract replaces {contract['filename']}, which also "
      "covers " + ", ".join(sorted({course for course, _ in missing}))
      + "; include those courses to keep their sessions accounted for.")
@

The same goes for the dates: a replaced contract may cover more than
this contract's period.
Say the TA has a contract for the whole autumn and we create one for
period~2.
The two overlap, so the new one replaces the autumn contract, and the
period~1 sessions lose the contract that paid for them.
Carrying then moves all of them into period~2, and the percentage
rises steeply; without a carry window they show up on the next time
sheet instead.
That is right only if the autumn contract was wrong.
More likely the autumn contract was right, and the TA shouldn't get a
period~2 contract at all.

Only the sessions in the replaced contract's own period count here.
A contract that carried also holds sessions outside its period, the
carried ones, by construction.
A new version of it, with the same period and booking window, carries
them again, so nothing is lost; and [[show --updates]] makes such a
redo for every contract it shows, so warning about them would be noise.
A new version that doesn't reach them with its booking window, however,
drops them: they go back to the time sheet.
That may well be intended, but it deserves a warning of its own.
<<warn about replaced contracts for sessions outside this period>>=
for contract in user_contracts:
  if contract in kept_contracts:
    continue
  rows = contract["events"] + contract.get("removed_events", [])
  own_rows = rows_in_window(rows, contract_window(contract))
  carried_rows = [row for row in rows if row not in own_rows]
  <<warn if [[contract]] covers sessions outside this period>>
  <<warn if [[contract]] carried sessions this one doesn't reach>>
@

A replaced contract's sessions outside our period are those of its own
period that ours doesn't cover.
<<warn if [[contract]] covers sessions outside this period>>=
in_our_period = rows_in_window(own_rows, own_window)
lost = [row for row in own_rows if row not in in_our_period]
if lost:
  count, dates = count_and_span(lost)
  logging.warning(
    f"{user}: this contract replaces {contract['filename']}, which also "
    f"covers {count} outside this contract's period ({dates}). No "
    "contract pays for them any more, so they are carried into this one "
    "or left for the time sheet. If that isn't intended, don't let this "
    "contract's period overlap that contract's.")
@

The carried sessions this contract doesn't reach are those outside its
booking window, which [[filter_events_by_date]] decides as it does for
the bookings themselves.
<<warn if [[contract]] carried sessions this one doesn't reach>>=
reached = sheets.filter_events_by_date(carried_rows,
                                       bookings_start, bookings_end)
dropped = [row for row in carried_rows if row not in reached]
if dropped:
  count, dates = count_and_span(dropped)
  logging.warning(
    f"{user}: this contract replaces {contract['filename']}, which "
    f"carried {count} ({dates}) that this contract's booking window "
    "doesn't reach; "
    + ("it is" if len(dropped) == 1 else "they are")
    + " left for the time sheet.")
@

The list of sessions can be long, so the warnings name only how many
there are and the dates of the first and last.
<<helper functions>>=
def count_and_span(rows):
  """
  Returns ("N sessions", "first to last") for the sign-up sheet `rows`,
  with the dates of the first and last session.
  """
  rows = sorted(rows, key=lambda row: row[1])
  count = f"{len(rows)} session" + ("s" if len(rows) != 1 else "")
  if len(rows) == 1:
    return count, rows[0][1]
  return count, f"{rows[0][1]} to {rows[-1][1]}"
@

One session is \enquote{1 session}, and several are spanned from the
earliest to the latest whatever their order.
<<test functions>>=
def test_count_and_span():
  first = _row("2025-09-01 10:00", "2025-09-01 12:00")
  middle = _row("2025-09-08 10:00", "2025-09-08 12:00")
  last = _row("2025-09-15 10:00", "2025-09-15 12:00")
  assert count_and_span([middle]) == ("1 session", middle[1])
  assert count_and_span([middle, last, first]) == \
    ("3 sessions", f"{first[1]} to {last[1]}")
@

A kept contract with events inside this contract's period must have
carried them there, since its own period doesn't overlap ours.
That's the third case above.
<<warn if [[kept_contracts]] carried sessions from this period>>=
for contract in kept_contracts:
  carried = contract["events"] + contract.get("removed_events", [])
  if rows_in_window(carried, own_window):
    logging.warning(f"{user}: {contract['filename']} carried sessions "
                    "from this contract's period. Re-create that "
                    "contract after this one, so that the sessions "
                    "aren't counted twice.")
@

The difference changes the percentage, so we say what was carried.
We list the dates rather than just counting the sessions, since that's
what lets us check the result against the sign-up sheet.
<<report the carried difference for [[user]]>>=
if added_rows or removed_rows:
  logging.warning(
    f"{user}: carrying into the contract: "
    f"added {', '.join(row[1] for row in added_rows) or 'none'}; "
    f"removed {', '.join(row[1] for row in removed_rows) or 'none'}.")
@

The planning spreadsheet must count the same sessions as the contract,
so it gets the TA's bookings per course restricted to [[user_booked]].
The removed sessions have no place in its per-course rows, so the
spreadsheet shows the sessions worked, not the deduction.
<<let [[user_booked_by_course]] be [[booked_by_course]] for [[user]]>>=
user_booked_by_course = {
    key: [row for row in rows if row in user_booked]
    for key, rows in booked_by_course.items()
}
@

Finally, the stored contract must let us recompute it and check it.
We store the courses we read, as [[(course, register)]] pairs, for the
course check of later carries (\cref{sec:carry-courses}) and to let
[[show --updates]] read the same registers again.
The removed sessions are part of the contract, and we also keep the
booking window: the stored [[start]] and [[end]] are the contract's
dates (\cref{sec:contract-dates}), so without the booking window,
re-computing the contract from current bookings in [[show --updates]]
couldn't know that it was meant to carry anything.
<<contract fields for the carried difference>>=
"courses": sorted(list(key) for key in courses),
"bookings_start": bookings_start.isoformat() if bookings_start else None,
"bookings_end": bookings_end.isoformat() if bookings_end else None,
"removed_events": removed_rows,
@

Let's verify the whole round trip through the real commands.
[[erin]] had a period~1 contract for two sessions; she then dropped the
second to [[frank]] and took an extra, longer session.
We first check that a period~1 time sheet would report that difference,
so that the final check isn't vacuous; then we create the period~2
contract carrying it, and check that the time sheet reports nothing.
Only the network-bound pieces are stubbed (the course configuration and
the sign-up sheet download) along with the configuration of the two
directories; the contract computation is real.
[[erin]] has a contract in the period, so her difference is
\enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{mertid}} and the time sheet is run
with [[--amanuensis]] (\cref{sec:timesheets-amanuensis}).
<<test functions>>=
def _erin_scenario(tmp_path, monkeypatch, p1_regex="DD1310"):
  """
  Sets up erin's P1 contract (a session `kept`, a session `missed` since
  given to frank, and enough regular sessions for a contract of its own)
  and a sign-up sheet with an extra session and enough P2 sessions for a
  contract. Returns (kept, missed, extra) rows.
  """
  import typerconf
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli

  contracts_dir = tmp_path / "contracts"
  contracts_dir.mkdir()
  timesheets_dir = tmp_path / "timesheets"
  def mock_config_get(key):
    if key == "hr.amanuensis.contract_dir":
      return str(contracts_dir)
    if key == "hr.timesheets.timesheets_dir":
      return str(timesheets_dir)
    raise KeyError(key)
  monkeypatch.setattr(typerconf, "get", mock_config_get)

  kept = _row("2025-09-01 10:00", "2025-09-01 12:00")
  missed = _row("2025-09-08 10:00", "2025-09-08 12:00")
  regular = [_sheet_row(f"2025-09-{day:02} 13:00", f"2025-09-{day:02} 17:00")
             for day in range(16, 24)]
  _write_contract(contracts_dir, "erin", "2025-08-01T10:00:00", *P1,
                  events=[kept, missed] + [row[:5] + ["erin"]
                                           for row in regular],
                  course_regex=p1_regex)

  extra = _sheet_row("2025-09-15 10:00", "2025-09-15 13:00")
  p2_sessions = [_sheet_row(f"2025-11-{day:02} 10:00",
                            f"2025-11-{day:02} 14:00")
                 for day in range(3, 28)]
  booked = [_sheet_row("2025-09-01 10:00", "2025-09-01 12:00"),
            _sheet_row("2025-09-08 10:00", "2025-09-08 12:00",
                       tas=("frank",)),
            extra] + regular + p2_sessions
  config = typerconf.Config({"signupsheet": {"url": "https://example.com/s.csv"},
                             "code": "DD1310"})
  registers = {"DD1310": {("DD1310", "reg"): config},
               "(DD1310|DD1337)": {("DD1310", "reg"): config,
                                   ("DD1337", "reg"): config}}
  monkeypatch.setattr(hrcli.coursescli, "get_courses_configs",
                      lambda regex, *a, **k: registers[regex])
  monkeypatch.setattr(nytid.signup.sheets, "read_signup_sheet_from_url",
                      lambda url: booked)
  monkeypatch.chdir(tmp_path)
  return kept, missed, extra

ERIN_TIMESHEET = ["timesheets", "generate", "DD1310", "--user", "erin",
                  "--start", "2025-08-25", "--end", "2025-10-26",
                  "--amanuensis"]
ERIN_P2_CARRYING = ["amanuensis", "create", "DD1310", "--user", "erin",
                    "--no-detailed",
                    "--start", "2025-08-25", "--end", "2026-01-18",
                    "--set-start", "2025-10-27", "--set-end", "2026-01-18"]
ERIN_P2_ALONE = ERIN_P2_CARRYING[:6] + ["--start", "2025-10-27"] \
              + ERIN_P2_CARRYING[8:]
ERIN_P1 = ["amanuensis", "create", "DD1310", "--user", "erin",
           "--no-detailed",
           "--start", "2025-08-25", "--end", "2025-10-26",
           "--set-start", "2025-08-25", "--set-end", "2025-10-26"]

def _printed_hours(output):
  """Returns the contract hours printed by create with --event-summary."""
  for line in output.splitlines():
    fields = line.split("\t")
    if fields[0] == "erin" and len(fields) == 5:
      return float(fields[4].removesuffix(" h"))
  raise AssertionError(f"no contract line in {output!r}")

def _hours(rows):
  """Returns erin's amanuensis hours for `rows`, prep time included."""
  return nytid.signup.hr.compute_amanuensis_data(
    rows, low_percentage=0, min_days=0)["erin"][2]

def _erin_p2_contract():
  p2 = [c for c in get_user_contracts("erin")
        if c["start"].startswith("2025-10-27")]
  assert len(p2) == 1
  return p2[0]

def test_carried_difference_leaves_nothing_for_time_sheets(tmp_path,
                                                           monkeypatch):
  from typer.testing import CliRunner
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli
  kept, missed, extra = _erin_scenario(tmp_path, monkeypatch)

  before = CliRunner().invoke(hrcli.cli, ERIN_TIMESHEET)
  assert before.exit_code == 0, before.output
  assert "erin" in before.stdout

  result = CliRunner().invoke(hrcli.cli, ERIN_P2_CARRYING)
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output

  p2 = _erin_p2_contract()
  assert extra[:5] + ["erin"] in p2["events"]
  assert kept not in p2["events"]
  assert p2["removed_events"] == [missed]
  assert len(get_user_contracts("erin")) == 2

  after = CliRunner().invoke(hrcli.cli, ERIN_TIMESHEET)
  assert after.exit_code == 0, after.output
  assert "erin" not in after.stdout
@

The point of carrying is the contract's hours, and the time-sheet check
above can't see them.
The carrying contract must have exactly the hours of period~2 alone plus
those of the added session minus those of the missed one.
We compute the latter two with [[compute_amanuensis_data]] directly, so
that the expectation doesn't depend on the code under test.
<<test functions>>=
def test_carried_difference_changes_the_hours(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
  from typer.testing import CliRunner
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli
  kept, missed, extra = _erin_scenario(tmp_path, monkeypatch)

  alone = CliRunner().invoke(hrcli.cli,
                             ERIN_P2_ALONE + ["--draft", "--event-summary"])
  carrying = CliRunner().invoke(hrcli.cli,
                                ERIN_P2_CARRYING + ["--event-summary"])
  assert alone.exit_code == 0 and carrying.exit_code == 0, carrying.output
  assert _printed_hours(carrying.stdout) == pytest.approx(
    _printed_hours(alone.stdout) + _hours([extra]) - _hours([missed]))
@

Re-creating the contract for period~2 must carry the same difference
again: the old version is replaced, so what it carried mustn't count as
accounted for.
<<test functions>>=
def test_recreated_contract_carries_again(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
  from typer.testing import CliRunner
  from nytid.cli import hr as hrcli
  kept, missed, extra = _erin_scenario(tmp_path, monkeypatch)

  for _ in range(2):
    result = CliRunner().invoke(hrcli.cli, ERIN_P2_CARRYING)
    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output

  newest = sorted((tmp_path / "contracts").glob("erin.*.json"))[-1]
  p2 = _erin_p2_contract()
  assert p2["filename"] == newest.name
  assert extra[:5] + ["erin"] in p2["events"]
  assert p2["removed_events"] == [missed]
