The valid contract is the latest contract in a series of overlapping contracts.
A newer contract replaces every older one whose period overlaps its
own, so we walk the contracts from the newest to the oldest
and keep a contract only if no newer contract overlaps it.

Two simpler walks suggest themselves, and both are wrong.
An earlier version sorted the files oldest first and compared each
contract only with the one just before it.
That works for a single chain of versions, but not when contracts for
different periods are interleaved in time.
Say we create a contract for period~1, then one for period~2, and then
redo the one for period~1.
The first version of period~1 is followed by period~2, which doesn't
overlap it, so it was kept---alongside the new version that was meant
to replace it.
Comparing against the newer contracts we have \emph{kept}, rather than
against \emph{all} newer contracts, goes wrong in the opposite
direction: if a contract spanning periods~1 and~2 is replaced by one
covering only period~2, the old period~1 contract would come back to
life, although the spanning contract had already replaced it.
Comparing with every newer contract gives each replacement its effect
once and for all.

Remember that a TA can have several valid contracts:
they might have had a contract for the autumn semester and then got a new one
for the spring semester.
Both of those contracts are considered valid since their periods don't
overlap.

\subsubsection{Loading contracts from disk}\label{loading-contracts}

When reading a contract from a file, we load the JSON data and augment it with
the filename.
The filename is crucial for timestamp-based comparisons, as explained earlier.
Without storing it, we wouldn't be able to determine which version of a 
contract is most recent when filtering for current contracts.
<<read [[contract]] from [[contract_file]]>>=
with open(contract_file) as infile:
  contract = json.load(infile)
contract["filename"] = contract_file.name
@

Now we can implement the logic to add valid contracts for a user.
The filenames sort in the order the contracts were created, so reversing
them puts the newest first.
<<add the valid contracts for [[user]] to [[contracts]]>>=
user_contracts = []
newer_contracts = []

contract_files = sorted(contracts_path.glob(f"{user}.*.json"),
                        key=lambda x: x.name, reverse=True)
for contract_file in contract_files:
  try:
    <<read [[contract]] from [[contract_file]]>>
  except Exception as err:
    logging.warning(f"Can't read {contract_file}, skipping: {err}")
    continue

  if not any(contract_overlap(contract, newer)
             for newer in newer_contracts):
    user_contracts.append(contract)

  newer_contracts.append(contract)

contracts += reversed(user_contracts)
@

We add the user's contracts oldest first, as the earlier walk did, so
that the order of the output doesn't change.

\subsubsection{When two contracts overlap}

Two contracts overlap when the periods they claim overlap
(\cref{sec:contract-dates}).
An earlier version said they overlap when they share a teaching event.
That rule has two holes.
First, a new version of a contract need not share any event with the old
one: if the TA's bookings change completely, the two versions have
disjoint events and both would count, paying the TA twice for the same
period.
Second, a contract may legitimately contain events from outside its own
period: when we carry the difference from an earlier period into the
next contract (\cref{sec:carry-difference}), the period~2 contract holds
sessions from period~1.
Under the event rule, that contract would silently replace the period~1
contract.
The periods are what the employment is about, so they are what decides.
<<helper functions>>=
def contract_overlap(contract1, contract2):
  """
  Returns True if the periods the contracts claim overlap, False
  otherwise.
  """
  return windows_overlap(contract_window(contract1),
                         contract_window(contract2))
@

The periods have day resolution and are inclusive at both ends, so a
contract ending on the 26th and one starting on the 27th don't overlap.
A missing bound means the period is open in that direction.
<<helper functions>>=
def windows_overlap(window1, window2):
  """
  Returns True if the date windows overlap. Each window is a pair
  (start, end) of `datetime.date` objects, inclusive; None means open.
  """
  start1, end1 = window1
  start2, end2 = window2
  return (start1 is None or end2 is None or start1 <= end2) \
     and (start2 is None or end1 is None or start2 <= end1)
@

