To compute the difference in salary, we can simply sum up the salary in the 
events.
The function [[summarize_user]] lets [[belopp]] key of the event be the salary.
<<helper functions>>=
def salary_difference(added_events, removed_events):
  """
  Returns the difference in salary between the added and removed events.
  """
  return sum(map(lambda x: x["belopp"], added_events)) \
           - sum(map(lambda x: x["belopp"], removed_events))
@

\subsection{Work with amanuensis or hourly TAs option}
\label{sec:timesheets-amanuensis}

We want to be able to separate time sheets for amanuensis and hourly TAs.
An amanuensis reports the sessions worked beyond the contract as
\enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{mertid}} instead.
<<option [[amanuensis]] to include amanuensis mertid>>=
amanuensis: Annotated[bool, amanuensis_opt] = False
<<argument and option definitions>>=
amanuensis_opt = typer.Option(help="Include only amanuenses, i.e. generate "
                                   "mertid time sheets. no-amanuensis "
                                   "include only hourly TAs.")
@

Which time sheet a TA belongs on depends on their contracts, and the
question is when.
Contrast the two simple answers, which both put TAs on the wrong time
sheet:
\begin{description}
\item[Today] The monthly jobs run in the first days of the next month,
  so a contract that ended with the month is missed, and the extra
  sessions in it go on an hourly time sheet.
\item[In the time sheet's period] The jobs give only [[--end]], and
  their course pattern matches every round of a course, so the period
  reaches back over every contract the TA ever had.
  A former amanuensis would never get an hourly time sheet again.
\end{description}
What the time sheet is about is the sessions it reports, and the latest
of them tells what the TA is now.
So we ask whether a contract employed the TA on the day of the last
reported session.
Not on any of them: the sessions an earlier contract paid for are
accounted for, but an amanuensis's extra sessions stay in the
difference until a time sheet is stored, and the jobs don't store.
Asking about any reported session would make the former amanuensis one
for good again.
A TA with nothing to report gets no time sheet anyway, so we skip them
before asking.
<<skip depending on [[amanuensis]] option>>=
reported = added_events + removed_events
if not reported:
  continue
first, last = sessions_span(reported)
if amanuensis != employed_on(user, last):
  continue
<<warn if [[user]]'s sessions span an employment's start or end>>
@

The decision is for the whole TA, not for each session.
A TA has one time sheet of each kind, named after them, and the drafts
of [[nytid.premonthly.sh]] send a TA the mertid draft only when the
hourly one is empty, so each TA must be one or the other.
A TA whose sessions span the start or end of an employment therefore
has all of them on the time sheet of the last one.
That is rare, so we leave it to be checked by hand, but we must say so.
We compare the first session with the last, and warn only in the run
that includes the TA, so that the warning comes once and not from both
runs.
<<warn if [[user]]'s sessions span an employment's start or end>>=
if employed_on(user, first) != employed_on(user, last):
  logging.warning(f"{user}'s sessions from {first} to {last} span "
                  f"the start or end of an employment, all on the "
                  f"{'mertid' if amanuensis else 'hourly'} time sheet")
<<helper functions>>=
def sessions_span(events):
  """
  Returns the first and last day, as `datetime.date`, of the summarized
  `events` (dictionaries with a `datum` key, as from `summarize_user`),
  which must not be empty.
  """
  days = sorted(datetime.date.fromisoformat(event["datum"])
                for event in events)
  return days[0], days[-1]
@

Employed means the contract's employment dates, [[start]] and [[end]],
not the period it claims (\cref{sec:contract-dates}).
The claimed period runs from the first to the last session the contract
pays for, and it decides which contracts replace each other.
An amanuensis, however, is employed, and works extra, over the whole
employment, also after the last session of the contract.
A contract without both dates employs no one: like the listing of
contracts, we leave out what we can't date rather than let it cover
every day.

We must look up the TA's contracts with [[get_user_contracts]], which
matches the username exactly.
[[get_valid_contracts]] takes a regular expression and matches it from
the start of the username only, so [[ab]] would also get the contracts
of [[abc]].
