\subsection{Summarize the events for a TA}\label{sec:summarize-user}

The [[summarize_user]] function will take a user and the course events and
return a list that can be put into the time sheet.
This is essentially the bulk of the time sheet contents.

The prep-time policy differs between hourly TAs and amanuenses: an on-campus
lab session counts 1.8 times its clock hours for an hourly TA but only 1.5
times for an amanuensis (see [[prep_factor]] in \cref{HRaddprep}).
Both kinds of sheet are built from this one function, so it takes an
[[amanuensis]] flag and applies it to \emph{both} the coefficient and the
prep-included hours; if only one of them used the flag the two fields would
disagree about which policy the row follows.
The default is hourly, and it has to stay that way: the hourly time sheet
diffs summarized events against previously reported ones by dictionary
equality (see [[remove_events]]), so every summary that feeds that diff must
use the same factors.
Only the amanuensis planning sheet (\cref{sec:amanuensis-course-rows}) opts
in.
<<helper functions>>=
def summarize_user(user, course_events,
                   salary=165, amanuensis=False):
  """
  Returns events where TA worked.
  - `user` is the TA's username.
  - `course_events` is a list of events.
  - Optional `salary` is the hourly salary.
  - Optional `amanuensis` selects the amanuensis prep-time policy instead
    of the hourly one (affects `koeff` and `omr_tid`).
  """
  <<signup machinery imports>>
  <<[[summarize_user]] variables>>
  <<let [[events]] be a list of events for [[user]], sorted by date>>

  xl_events = []

  for event in events:
    <<compute [[end]], [[start]], [[event_type]]>>
    <<compute [[time]] and [[time_with_prep]] from [[start]] and [[end]]>>
    <<compute [[course]] from [[event_type]]>>

    xl_events.append({
            "datum": str(start.date()),
            "tid": str(start.time()),
            "kurskod": course,
            "typ": event_type,
            "timmar": to_hours(time),
            "koeff": hr.prep_factor(event_type, date=start.date(),
                                    amanuensis=amanuensis),
            "omr_tid": time_with_prep,
            "belopp": time_with_prep * salary
        })

  return xl_events
@


\subsection{Build course rows for amanuensis planning spreadsheet}%
\label{sec:amanuensis-course-rows}

The planning sheet wants one row per course, with hours split into the
template's fixed categories.  Because [[booked_by_course]] already keeps each
course's bookings separate, [[amanuensis_course_rows]] can walk the courses one
at a time: for each it summarises the user's events, folds them into the
template's columns, and labels the row with the course's official code,
division, and study period from configuration.

Taking the course code from configuration --- rather than parsing it out of the
event title --- is what makes this correct for every course.  The title is free
text and often carries no recognisable code: a course whose events are merely
named \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{Övning}} has nothing to parse, so a
title-based grouping would drop every such course into one blank-coded row (the
bug this design replaces).  The configured [[code]] is instead the exact
identifier the official sheet expects, and grouping by source course keeps
distinct courses on distinct rows even when their titles look alike.

The fold must preserve one invariant: the template computes each row's
\enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{Summa timmar}} as
$\mathit{koeff}\cdot\mathit{tim}+\dotsb$, and that must equal the course's
total \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{omräknad tid}} as our own functions
compute it.  Each step below is written to maintain it.
<<helper functions>>=
def amanuensis_course_rows(user, booked_by_course, courses_configs,
                           responsible_name=""):
  """
  Build course rows for an amanuensis planning spreadsheet.

  Produces one per-course dict for each course the user worked on, holding
  the fields the planning template needs. Preserves the invariant that each
  row's Summa timmar equals that course's total omraknad tid.

  Args:
      user: TA username.
      booked_by_course: dict {(course, register): rows} of booked sign-up
          sheet rows, grouped by the course they came from.
      courses_configs: dict {(course, register): config} as returned by
          get_courses_configs, used for the course code, division and period.
      responsible_name: name for each row's responsible-teacher column.

  Returns:
      list of per-course dicts with keys: kurskod, avdelning, period,
      ovn_koeff, ovn_tim, gruppansvar, labb_koeff, labb_tim, mote,
      rattning, ovrigt, responsible.
  """
  <<map from event-type keyword to template column>>
  course_rows = []
  for (course, register), course_bookings in booked_by_course.items():
    <<summarise [[user]]'s events in this course, skip if none>>
    <<classify the course's events into template columns>>
    <<fold the columns into one course row>>
    <<label the row with code, division and period>>
    course_rows.append(row)
  return course_rows
@

The template's columns are fixed (exercises, labs, group responsibility,
meetings, grading, other) while our event types are free text.  We map a type
to a column by the same keywords [[prep_factor]] keys off, so the classification
agrees with how the coefficient was assigned.

\enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{Gruppansvar}} has no prep-time coefficient
of its own, so it is a flat column like meetings and grading: an event whose
type names it contributes its hours directly.  Without an explicit keyword it
would silently fall into \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{övrigt}} (its hours
would still count toward the row total, just in the wrong column), so we map it
by name.
<<map from event-type keyword to template column>>=
category_map = {
  "övning": "ovn", "exercise": "ovn",
  "laboration": "labb", "labb": "labb", "lab": "labb",
  "seminar": "labb", "redovisning": "labb", "presentation": "labb",
  "frågestund": "labb", "question": "labb",
  "gruppansvar": "gruppansvar", "group responsib": "gruppansvar",
  "möte": "mote", "meeting": "mote",
  "rättning": "rattning", "grading": "rattning", "correction": "rattning",
}
@

We reuse [[summarize_user]], which keeps only the events this TA was booked
(not reserve) for and tags each with its clock hours and prep-included hours.
This sheet plans an amanuensis contract, so we ask for the amanuensis
prep-time policy: that is the policy [[compute_amanuensis_data]] used to
compute the contract's hours in the first place, and it gives labs their 1.5
coefficient rather than the hourly 1.8.
With the default (hourly) policy the sheet would both show the wrong lab
coefficient and sum to more hours than the contract it accompanies.
A course the TA never actually worked yields no events, so we skip it rather
than emit an empty row.
<<summarise [[user]]'s events in this course, skip if none>>=
events = summarize_user(user, course_bookings, amanuensis=True)
if not events:
  continue
@

We then sort each event into a template column.  An unrecognised type falls
into \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{övrigt}} rather than being dropped, so
the total survives an imperfect mapping.
<<classify the course's events into template columns>>=
categorized = {"ovn": [], "labb": [], "mote": [],
               "rattning": [], "ovrigt": [], "gruppansvar": []}
for event in events:
  typ = event["typ"].lower()
  category = "ovrigt"
  for key, value in category_map.items():
    if key in typ:
      category = value
      break
  categorized[category].append(event)
@

Now we fold each category into the template's input cells.  The two
coefficient-bearing columns store raw clock hours times a coefficient, so we
write the summed clock hours and an \emph{effective} coefficient
$\sum\mathit{omr\_tid}/\sum\mathit{tim}$ --- which makes
$\mathit{koeff}\cdot\mathit{tim}$ reproduce the summed prep-included hours
exactly.  The flat columns have no coefficient, so we write the prep-included
hours directly.  Either way the column contributes its share of
\enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{omräknad tid}}, so the row total matches.
<<fold the columns into one course row>>=
row = {
  "kurskod": "", "avdelning": "", "period": "",
  "ovn_koeff": 0, "ovn_tim": 0, "gruppansvar": 0,
  "labb_koeff": 0, "labb_tim": 0, "mote": 0,
  "rattning": 0, "ovrigt": 0, "responsible": responsible_name,
}

for category, cat_events in categorized.items():
  if category in ("ovn", "labb"):
    total_timmar = sum(e["timmar"] for e in cat_events)
    total_omr_tid = sum(e["omr_tid"] for e in cat_events)
    if total_timmar > 0:
      row[f"{category}_koeff"] = total_omr_tid / total_timmar
      row[f"{category}_tim"] = total_timmar
  else:
    row[category] = sum(e["omr_tid"] for e in cat_events)
@

Finally we label the row from this course's own configuration: the official
[[code]], plus the division and study period under [[hr.division]] and
[[hr.period]].  When [[code]] is unset we fall back to the course's own
identifier, so the row is still labelled with something recognisable rather
than left blank.  Course configurations use dotted keys and raise [[KeyError]]
when a key is unset, so we read them defensively through [[course_config_get]].
<<label the row with code, division and period>>=
config = courses_configs.get((course, register))
row["kurskod"] = course_config_get(config, "code", "") or course
row["avdelning"] = course_config_get(config, "hr.division", "")
row["period"] = course_config_get(config, "hr.period", "")
@

The accessor reads a dotted configuration key, returning a default instead of
raising when the key is unset --- and treats a missing configuration ([[None]])
as all-unset, so a course absent from [[courses_configs]] yields defaults rather
than an [[AttributeError]].
<<helper functions>>=
def course_config_get(config, key, default=None):
  """Return the value at dotted `key` in `config`, or `default` if unset."""
  if config is None:
    return default
  try:
    return config.get(key)
  except KeyError:
    return default
@

The invariant we care about is that the spreadsheet, fed our numbers, computes
the same totals we do.  We pin it with a test: we build a couple of courses'
worth of events, grouped by course the way the command supplies them, run them
through [[amanuensis_course_rows]], evaluate the template's hour formula
($\mathit{koeff}\cdot\mathit{tim}+\dotsb$) in Python, and check it equals the
total \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{omräknad tid}} that [[summarize_user]]
reports for each course under the amanuensis policy.  We also confirm that
each course yields its own row and that exercise and lab hours land in their
own columns.
<<test functions>>=
def _amanuensis_signup_row(event, start, end, tas, needed="1"):
  return [event, start, end, "D1", needed] + tas

def test_amanuensis_course_rows_total_matches():
  dd1310 = [
    _amanuensis_signup_row("DD1310 Övning",
                           "2024-09-02 10:00", "2024-09-02 12:00", ["alice"]),
    _amanuensis_signup_row("DD1310 Laboration",
                           "2024-09-03 10:00", "2024-09-03 13:00", ["alice"]),
  ]
  dd1317 = [
    _amanuensis_signup_row("DD1317 Möte",
                           "2024-09-04 10:00", "2024-09-04 11:00", ["alice"]),
  ]
  booked_by_course = {("DD1310", "reg"): dd1310, ("DD1317", "reg"): dd1317}
  course_rows = amanuensis_course_rows("alice", booked_by_course, {})

  # With no config the kurskod falls back to the course identifier.
  assert {r["kurskod"] for r in course_rows} == {"DD1310", "DD1317"}

  expected = {
    "DD1310": sum(e["omr_tid"] for e in
                  summarize_user("alice", dd1310, amanuensis=True)),
    "DD1317": sum(e["omr_tid"] for e in
                  summarize_user("alice", dd1317, amanuensis=True)),
  }

  for r in course_rows:
    sheet_total = (r["ovn_koeff"] * r["ovn_tim"]
                   + r["gruppansvar"]
                   + r["labb_koeff"] * r["labb_tim"]
                   + r["mote"] + r["rattning"] + r["ovrigt"])
    assert sheet_total == pytest.approx(expected[r["kurskod"]])

  row1310 = next(r for r in course_rows if r["kurskod"] == "DD1310")
  assert row1310["ovn_tim"] == 2    # the Övning clock hours
  assert row1310["labb_tim"] == 3   # the Laboration clock hours
@

The invariant above holds under either policy, so it cannot tell them apart.
We therefore pin the policy itself: an on-campus lab in 2024 must get the
amanuensis coefficient 1.5 in the planning sheet, while [[summarize_user]]'s
default must still yield the hourly 1.8 --- the hourly time sheets, and the
diff they rely on, depend on that default not moving.
(The hourly prep-included hours are $3.75$ rather than $3.6$ because
[[add_prep_time]] rounds up to the quarter hour after scaling; at 1.5 the
product is exact.)
<<test functions>>=
def test_amanuensis_course_rows_uses_amanuensis_lab_factor():
  lab = [
    _amanuensis_signup_row("Laboration",
                           "2024-09-03 10:00", "2024-09-03 12:00", ["alice"]),
  ]
  course_rows = amanuensis_course_rows("alice", {("DD1310", "reg"): lab}, {})

  assert len(course_rows) == 1
  assert course_rows[0]["labb_tim"] == 2
  assert course_rows[0]["labb_koeff"] == pytest.approx(1.5)

  hourly = summarize_user("alice", lab)
  assert hourly[0]["koeff"] == pytest.approx(1.8)
  assert hourly[0]["omr_tid"] == pytest.approx(3.75)

  amanuensis = summarize_user("alice", lab, amanuensis=True)
  assert amanuensis[0]["koeff"] == pytest.approx(1.5)
  assert amanuensis[0]["omr_tid"] == pytest.approx(3.0)

def test_amanuensis_course_rows_code_comes_from_config():
