\section{Tracking who is reported to HR as employed}%
\label{EmployedTracking}

Before a TA can work, we must report them to HR, who register them as
an employee.
For amanuenses that reporting leaves a trace in nytid: the stored
contract covers a date range, so \enquote{is this TA known to HR?} is
answered by looking for a current contract
(\cref{contract-filtering}).
Hourly TAs, however, are defined negatively---an hourly TA is simply a
booked TA \emph{without} a contract---so nothing records that they were
ever reported.
The weekly cron script (\cref{CLIoverview}) used to fill this gap by diffing
the output of [[nytid hr users --detailed]] against plain-text files,
keyed on the whole rendered line: a changed email address made a TA
look new again, and nothing recorded when or what was reported.

We close the asymmetry by giving hourly TAs a positive record of their
own: one JSON file per reporting event, in the same
one-file-per-event, glob-to-query style as the contracts and the time
sheets.
A TA then counts as covered if they have a current or upcoming
amanuensis contract, or a current employment record.
Everything lives under its own subcommand group:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[missing]] ] lists the booked TAs who are covered by neither,
  \ie who still need to be reported to HR.
\item[ [[report]] ] does the same, but also records each TA as
  reported and prints the identity line that goes to HR.
\item[ [[list]] ] shows the stored records.
\item[ [[ignore]] ] stores records that suppress reporting, \eg for
  colleagues who sign up on the course but are already employed.
\end{description}
<<subcommands>>=
employed = typer.Typer(name="employed",
                       help="Track who is reported to HR as employed")
cli.add_typer(employed)
@

\subsection{The employment record store}

The records must be shared: any teacher on the course should be able
to see that a TA is already reported.
For the contracts and time sheets we get sharing by pointing the
configured directory into AFS, and the same holds here.
But unlike [[timesheets_directory]], which returns a bare
[[pathlib.Path]], we access the store through [[storage.open_root]]
(\cref{storage}).
That function picks the storage backend from the path alone---plain
directory, AFS, or a Git repository---so moving the store off AFS
later (say, to GitHub) is a config change, not a code change.
The price is small: callers open the root in a [[with]] statement and
open files relative to it, instead of joining paths themselves.
<<helper functions>>=
def employment_storage():
  """
  Returns a StorageRoot for the employment record store.

  The location is read from the config key
  `hr.employment.employment_dir`; falls back to `./` with a warning
  when the key isn't configured.
  """
  try:
    path = typerconf.get(EMPLOYMENT_DIR_PATH)
  except KeyError:
    logging.warning(f"Can't find {EMPLOYMENT_DIR_PATH} in config, "
                    f"using `./`. Set by running "
                    f"`nytid config {EMPLOYMENT_DIR_PATH} -s <path>`.")
    path = "./"
  return storage.open_root(str(path))
<<constants>>=
EMPLOYMENT_DIR_PATH = "hr.employment.employment_dir"
<<imports>>=
from nytid import storage
@

The fallback mirrors [[timesheets_directory]]: warn, quote the
[[nytid config]] invocation that sets the key, and use the working
directory rather than crash on a fresh install.
<<test functions>>=
def test_employment_storage_falls_back_when_unconfigured(monkeypatch):
  import typerconf

  def unset(key):
    raise KeyError(key)

  monkeypatch.setattr(typerconf, "get", unset)

  assert employment_storage().path == pathlib.Path("./")

def test_employment_storage_uses_config(monkeypatch, tmp_path):
  import typerconf
  monkeypatch.setattr(typerconf, "get", lambda key: str(tmp_path))

  assert employment_storage().path == tmp_path
