Each record snapshots what we sent to HR and when.
We store the rendered identity line (personnummer, name, email) rather
than only the username: HR received exactly that line, and when a
question comes up months later we want to answer \enquote{what did I
send, and when?} without re-querying Canvas and LADOK, whose answers
may have changed since.
The [[valid_until]] date implements per-record staleness and is
explained with [[record_is_current]] below; [[ignored]] marks records
that exist only to suppress reporting.
The filename follows the contracts and time sheets,
[[user.timestamp.json]], so the same filename-based querying works:
the username is recoverable without opening the file, and ISO
timestamps sort lexicographically, so the newest record per user is
the last one in filename order.
<<helper functions>>=
def store_employment_record(root, user, identity, courses,
                            valid_until=None, ignored=False):
  """
  Records that `user` was reported to HR as employed.

  Stores a JSON record in the StorageRoot `root`, named
  `user.timestamp.json`. `identity` is the identity line sent to HR
  (or None), `courses` the courses that triggered the report,
  `valid_until` an optional expiry date (datetime.date) and `ignored`
  marks a record that only suppresses reporting. Returns the filename
  written.
  """
  now = datetime.datetime.now()
  filename = f"{user}.{now.isoformat()}.json"

  with root.open(filename, "w") as outfile:
    json.dump({"user": user,
               "reported": now.isoformat(),
               "valid_until":
                 valid_until.isoformat() if valid_until else None,
               "identity": identity,
               "courses": list(courses),
               "ignored": ignored},
              outfile,
              indent=2)

  return filename
@

We needn't create the directory ourselves the way [[store_timesheet]]
must: opening a [[StorageRoot]] creates a missing root
(\cref{storage}), so the first write on a fresh install works.
Let's verify that, and that the schema round-trips:
<<test functions>>=
def test_store_employment_record_creates_missing_directory(tmp_path):
  target = tmp_path / "does" / "not" / "exist"

  with storage.open_root(str(target)) as root:
    filename = store_employment_record(
      root, "alice", "[123] Alice <alice@kth.se>", ["prgi26"],
      valid_until=datetime.date(2027, 7, 1))

  with open(target / filename) as infile:
    stored = json.load(infile)

  assert stored["user"] == "alice"
  assert stored["identity"] == "[123] Alice <alice@kth.se>"
  assert stored["courses"] == ["prgi26"]
  assert stored["valid_until"] == "2027-07-01"
  assert not stored["ignored"]
@

\subsection{When does a record go stale?}

HR's registrations don't last forever: in practice they are reset over
summer, so a TA reported in autumn 2025 who returns in autumn 2026
must be reported again.
On the other hand, sometimes one simply clears out the directory of
old records (as one does with old contracts and time sheets), and an
emptied store must just mean that everyone is unreported again.
Both practices work because staleness is per record and the store is
queried by globbing at call time---there is no index or cache that
could disagree with the directory contents.

A record with no [[valid_until]] never goes stale; deleting it is the
only way to unreport such a TA.
A malformed date is treated as expired: the conservative direction,
since re-reporting an already-known TA is a minor annoyance while
silently never reporting one means they can't get paid.
<<helper functions>>=
def record_is_current(record, today=None):
  """
  Returns True if the employment record `record` is still valid.

  A record without a `valid_until` date never expires. `today`
  defaults to today's date and exists mainly for testing.
  """
  if today is None:
    today = datetime.date.today()

  valid_until = record.get("valid_until")
  if not valid_until:
    return True

  try:
    return datetime.date.fromisoformat(valid_until) >= today
  except ValueError as err:
    logging.warning(f"Malformed valid_until in "
                    f"{record.get('filename', record)}: {err}; "
                    f"treating the record as expired.")
    return False
@

Let's check both directions around the boundary, the no-expiry case
and the malformed-date policy:
<<test functions>>=
def test_record_is_current_no_expiry():
  assert record_is_current({"valid_until": None})
  assert record_is_current({})

def test_record_is_current_dates():
  today = datetime.date(2026, 8, 24)

  assert record_is_current({"valid_until": "2026-08-24"}, today=today)
  assert record_is_current({"valid_until": "2027-07-01"}, today=today)
  assert not record_is_current({"valid_until": "2026-07-01"},
                               today=today)

def test_record_is_current_malformed():
  assert not record_is_current({"valid_until": "not-a-date"})
@

Since the reset happens annually at roughly the same time, we don't
want to type the same [[--valid-until]] on every report.
Instead a config key holds the reset as a month and day, \eg
[[07-01]] for the first of July, and new records default to expiring
at the \emph{next} occurrence of that date.
We deliberately do not reuse the period machinery
([[hr.amanuensis.periods]]): those periods are contract periods, five
or so per year, and expiring records at period ends would flag every
TA for re-reporting several times a year when HR only resets
annually.
A month-day key matches the actual semantics and needs no per-year
maintenance.
When the key is unset, new records get no expiry, which keeps the
one-time interpretation for anyone who doesn't need staleness.
<<helper functions>>=
def default_valid_until(today=None):
  """
  Returns the default expiry date for a new employment record.

  This is the next occurrence, strictly after `today`, of the
  month-day in the config key `hr.employment.reset_date` (e.g.
  `07-01`). Returns None---no expiry---when the key is unset or
  malformed. `today` defaults to today's date.
  """
  if today is None:
    today = datetime.date.today()

  reset = default(EMPLOYMENT_RESET_DATE, None)
  if not reset:
    return None

  try:
    month, day = map(int, str(reset).split("-"))
    candidate = datetime.date(today.year, month, day)
    if candidate <= today:
      candidate = datetime.date(today.year + 1, month, day)
  except ValueError as err:
    logging.warning(f"Malformed {EMPLOYMENT_RESET_DATE} `{reset}`: "
                    f"{err}; new records get no expiry.")
    return None

  return candidate
<<constants>>=
EMPLOYMENT_RESET_DATE = "hr.employment.reset_date"
@

The interesting case is the year boundary: reporting in May expires
this year's first of July, while reporting in August expires next
year's.
[[default]] reads the cached personal config, so the tests substitute
it rather than the config file:
<<test functions>>=
def test_default_valid_until_unset(monkeypatch):
