\subsection{Reading the records back}

Querying works exactly like the contracts: the username is the first
dot-separated component of the filename, so we can filter users
without opening any file, and unreadable files are skipped with a
warning rather than taking the whole listing down with them.
We read through [[root.open]] so the storage backend stays
interchangeable, but glob via [[root.path]]---listing is a read-only
operation on the directory itself, which every backend exposes as a
local path.
<<helper functions>>=
def get_employment_records(root, user_regex=".*"):
  """
  Returns the employment records in the StorageRoot `root` for users
  matching `user_regex`.

  The records are sorted by username and then by age, oldest first,
  and each is augmented with its `filename`.
  """
  records = []
  user_pattern = re.compile(user_regex)

  for record_file in sorted(root.path.glob("*.json"),
                            key=lambda x: x.name):
    username = record_file.name.split(".")[0]
    if not user_pattern.match(username):
      continue

    try:
      with root.open(record_file.name) as infile:
        record = json.load(infile)
    except Exception as err:
      logging.warning(f"Can't read {record_file}, skipping: {err}")
      continue

    record["filename"] = record_file.name
    records.append(record)

  return records
@

The question the rest of this section needs answered is \enquote{who
is currently covered by an employment record?}.
Only the newest record per user counts: a newer record supersedes an
older one, so an old never-expiring record must not keep a user
covered when a newer, now-expired record shows they were re-reported
and reset since.
Because [[get_employment_records]] sorts by filename, the newest
record per user is simply the last one seen.
An ignored record covers its user just like a real one---that is the
whole point of ignoring---so we don't distinguish them here.
<<helper functions>>=
def employed_users(root, user_regex=".*", today=None):
  """
  Returns the set of users whose newest employment record in the
  StorageRoot `root` is current.

  Expired-newest counts the same as no record at all. Ignored records
  count as covering their user. `today` is passed on to
  `record_is_current`.
  """
  newest = {}
  for record in get_employment_records(root, user_regex):
    newest[record["user"]] = record

  return {user for user, record in newest.items()
          if record_is_current(record, today=today)}
@

The newest-record-wins rule and the emptied-store requirement are the
two behaviours worth pinning:
<<test functions>>=
def test_employed_users_newest_record_wins(tmp_path):
  with storage.open_root(str(tmp_path)) as root:
    store_employment_record(root, "alice", None, [])
    store_employment_record(root, "alice", None, [],
                            valid_until=datetime.date(2020, 1, 1))

    assert employed_users(root) == set()

def test_employed_users_ignored_counts_as_covered(tmp_path):
  with storage.open_root(str(tmp_path)) as root:
    store_employment_record(root, "bob", None, [], ignored=True)

    assert employed_users(root) == {"bob"}

def test_employed_users_empty_store(tmp_path):
  with storage.open_root(str(tmp_path)) as root:
    assert employed_users(root) == set()
