\subsection{Who is missing?}

With both stores queryable, coverage is set arithmetic.
The amanuensis side counts upcoming contracts too, not only current
ones: a contract that starts next period has already been through HR,
and flagging its holder would report them as hourly by mistake.
<<helper functions>>=
def contracted_users():
  """
  Returns the set of users covered by a current or upcoming
  amanuensis contract.
  """
  contracts = get_valid_contracts(".*", ContractFilterMode.CURRENT) \
            + get_valid_contracts(".*", ContractFilterMode.NEXT)
  return {contract["user"] for contract in contracts}
@

The missing TAs are the booked ones covered by neither store.
We keep this as a function of plain sets, so the rule is testable
without any storage or network:
<<helper functions>>=
def compute_missing(booked, contracted, employed):
  """
  Returns the set of users in `booked` that are in neither
  `contracted` nor `employed`.
  """
  return set(booked) - set(contracted) - set(employed)
<<test functions>>=
def test_compute_missing():
  booked = {"alice", "bob", "charlie", "dave"}

  assert compute_missing(booked, {"alice"}, {"bob"}) \
    == {"charlie", "dave"}
