\section{Checking reported hours in Medvind}%
\label{sec:hr-medvind}

Everything above starts from the bookings: who signed up, and
therefore what each TA should be paid for.
The other half of the picture is what the TAs themselves report in
Medvind, KTH's time-reporting system, and comparing the two is HR
work.
So the commands that read those reports belong here.

Two of them, [[nytid hr medvind plans]] and [[nytid hr medvind hours]],
only read Medvind and share no code with this module.
They live in a module and chapter of their own (\cref{cli.medvind}),
and we only mount their group.
That module names its Typer [[medvindcli]], not [[cli]], so the CLI's
start-up (\cref{climodule}) leaves it to us instead of also mounting it
at the top level.
<<imports>>=
from nytid.cli import medvind as medvindcmds
<<subcommands>>=
cli.add_typer(medvindcmds.medvindcli)
@

The third command, [[nytid hr medvind deviations]], compares the two
sides and lists the TAs whose reports do not match their bookings.
It is defined here rather than with the other two because it needs
both sides: the bookings, the contracts and the TAs' personal data
from this module, and the times from Medvind.
It is still a command of the [[medvind]] group, so we add it to
[[medvindcmds.medvindcli]].
The command is meant for a monthly cron job that mails the list
(\cref{CheckingReportedHours}), so its output says nothing at all when
everything matches.
\begin{minted}{bash}
nytid hr medvind deviations "(datintro|prg[im]|tilkry)"
\end{minted}


\subsection{What to compare}

What we compare follows from what we tell the TAs.
The time-sheet draft that the TAs get each month
(\cref{CLIoverview}) lists their bookings with the prep time included,
the salary hours ([[omr_tid]] from [[summarize_user]]), and asks them
to report those hours in Medvind.
So for each TA we compare the sum of [[omr_tid]] over the bookings in
the period with the sum of the hours they reported in Medvind.

Only hourly TAs report their hours this way.
An amanuensis is paid by contract, so a booking need not have a
matching report; we leave them out of the comparison.

On the Medvind side, the times that matter are those reported to us.
A plan holds the times of everyone in the organisation unit, and a TA
who also works for a colleague reports those hours too, with the
colleague as work leader.
So we select the times by work leader, [[--leader]], which is a regular
expression for our name as the TAs pick it in Medvind.
It has no sensible default, so it must be configured or given.

Finally, small differences are not worth an email.
Medvind counts in minutes, while our salary hours are the clock hours
times the prep factor, rounded up to a quarter of an hour
(\cref{HRroundtime}).
A TA who reports the unrounded time, $1.8 \cdot 2 = 3.6$ hours for a
two-hour lab instead of our $3.75$, is a few minutes off without
anything being wrong.
A difference counts only when it is at least [[--tolerance]] hours,
by default a quarter of an hour.
<<argument and option definitions>>=
MEDVIND_LEADER_CONFIG = "medvind.leader"
DEFAULT_MEDVIND_LEADER = default(MEDVIND_LEADER_CONFIG, None)
medvind_leader_opt = typer.Option(
  "--leader",
  help="Compare only times reported to a work leader matching this "
       "regex, normally your own name. "
       + config_hint(MEDVIND_LEADER_CONFIG))

MEDVIND_TOLERANCE_CONFIG = "medvind.tolerance"
DEFAULT_MEDVIND_TOLERANCE = default(MEDVIND_TOLERANCE_CONFIG, 0.25,
                                    cast=float)
medvind_tolerance_opt = typer.Option(
  "--tolerance",
  help="The smallest difference, in hours, that counts as a deviation. "
       + config_hint(MEDVIND_TOLERANCE_CONFIG))
@


\subsection{The command}

The command first settles the period, the same way as
[[nytid hr medvind hours]] (the previous month up to the cutoff day,
\cref{cli.medvind}), then collects the booked hours, then the reported
times, and prints the TAs that deviate.
We collect the bookings before logging in to Medvind, so that a
mistyped course regex fails at once instead of after a login.
<<subcommands>>=
@medvindcmds.medvindcli.command(name="deviations")
def cli_medvind_deviations(
    <<argument for matching courses>>,
    <<option for matching registers, default to mine>>,
    start: Annotated[Optional[datetime.datetime],
                     medvindcmds.from_opt] = None,
    end: Annotated[Optional[datetime.datetime],
                   medvindcmds.to_opt] = None,
    plan: Annotated[Optional[str], medvindcmds.plan_opt] = None,
    leader: Annotated[Optional[str],
                      medvind_leader_opt] = DEFAULT_MEDVIND_LEADER,
    tolerance: Annotated[float,
                         medvind_tolerance_opt] = DEFAULT_MEDVIND_TOLERANCE):
  """
  Lists the hourly TAs whose hours reported in Medvind differ from their
  booked hours (prep time included): user, name, booked, reported and
  the difference. Reporters without bookings have user `-`.

  Prints nothing when every TA's report matches.
  """
  import nytid.medvind as medvind

  <<let [[start]], [[end]] be the Medvind period>>
  <<exit unless we know the work [[leader]]>>
  <<set list [[courses]] to ((course, register), config)-pairs>>
  tas = booked_hourly_hours(courses, course_regex, start, end)
  connection = medvindcmds.connect()
  try:
    times = medvind.select(connection.timelist(start, end, plan),
                           leader=leader)
  except (ValueError, medvind.MedvindError) as err:
    logging.error(err)
    raise typer.Exit(1)
  except re.error as err:
    logging.error(f"Invalid regular expression {err.pattern!r}: {err}")
    raise typer.Exit(1)
  print_rows(["User", "Name", "Booked", "Reported", "Difference"],
             hours_deviations(tas, times, tolerance))
@

The period comes from [[nytid.cli.medvind]] through the module, not
as copied names, so that the module's one reading of the clock,
[[medvindcmds.today]], is the one a test fixes.
